"A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests at a local community event. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, unexpectedly, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?","A food caterer was told to prepare gourmet hot dogs for 36 guests. While most people would only eat one hotdog, he prepared enough for half of the guests to be able to have two hotdogs. However, 40 guests showed up, and everyone wanted a second hotdog. How many guests did not get a second hotdog?",26,26,26,26,"Let me solve this step by step.

1. Calculate how many hot dogs the caterer prepared:
   * Original expected guests = 36
   * Half of guests could have 2 hot dogs
   * So, 18 guests could have 2 hot dogs, and 18 would have 1 hot dog
   * Total hot dogs prepared = (18 × 2) + (18 × 1)
   * Total hot dogs prepared = 36 + 18 = 54 hot dogs

2. Calculate how many hot dogs were needed:
   * Actual number of guests = 40
   * Each guest wanted 2 hot dogs
   * Total hot dogs needed = 40 × 2 = 80 hot dogs

3. Calculate how many guests didn't get a second hot dog:
   * Hot dogs available = 54
   * First round used 40 hot dogs (one for each guest)
   * Remaining hot dogs = 54 - 40 = 14 hot dogs
   * Number of guests who got a second hot dog = 14
   * Number of guests who didn't get a second hot dog = 40 - 14 = 26

